# C / C++ security

Narrows `core-common-rules`' stack-agnostic security rules to C/C++
memory-safety and undefined-behavior risks, and the sanitizers/safe APIs to
use. Applies to both C and C++ files.

## Memory safety

- Never dereference, write through, or `free` a pointer after it was freed or
  left scope (use-after-free); set a freed pointer to `NULL` when it could be
  read again.
- Never `free`/`delete` the same pointer twice.
- Bounds-check every array/buffer index and every pointer arithmetic
  expression built from untrusted input before it reads or writes memory.
- Never `strcpy`, `strcat`, `sprintf`, or `gets` on data not statically known
  to fit; use `snprintf`, a length-checked copy, or `std::string`/
  `std::vector`.

## Undefined behavior

- Signed integer overflow is UB (unsigned wraparound is not): check bounds
  before a signed addition/multiplication, or use an unsigned/wider type or a
  checked-arithmetic helper, for size computations from untrusted input.
- Never read an uninitialized local or heap allocation; value-initialize
  (`Type x{};` in C++, `= 0`/`memset` in C).
- No strict-aliasing violations; use `memcpy`, or `std::bit_cast` (C++20+),
  for type punning.
- No out-of-bounds read past the last element via pointer arithmetic.

## Concurrency

- Every variable read by more than one thread while one writes it is guarded
  by a mutex or is `std::atomic`/C11 `_Atomic`.
- Acquire locks in a consistent global order; prefer `std::lock_guard`/
  `std::scoped_lock` (the latter locks several mutexes deadlock-free) over
  manual `lock()`/`unlock()` pairs.

## Sanitizers

- Build and run with AddressSanitizer + UndefinedBehaviorSanitizer
  (`-fsanitize=address,undefined -fno-omit-frame-pointer -g`, Clang/GCC)
  when investigating a memory-safety or UB report, or before trusting a fix
  for one.
- Build and run with ThreadSanitizer (`-fsanitize=thread`) as a separate
  binary from ASan/UBSan, since the runtimes are not composable, when
  investigating a data race or before trusting a fix for one.
- A memory-safety, UB, or concurrency bug is not verified fixed until it is
  reproduced and re-run clean under the relevant sanitizer.

## Dependency and input hygiene

- Validate the length/format of any externally-sourced buffer (network, file,
  IPC, command-line argument) before parsing; check a length field in the
  input against the buffer's actual size.
- No hard-coded credential, API key, or signing secret; load from the
  environment/secret storage the project already uses.
